WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.934
Your code reads Limits.CAP.

2
00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:07.748
The JVM has never seen this class.

3
00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:13.669
Its bytes sit in a jar, and the JVM reads none of them until some code needs one.

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.320
A class loader finds a class's bytes and turns them into a Class.

5
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.627
The JDK has three.

6
00:00:20.627 --> 00:00:22.516
Bootstrap loads java.base.

7
00:00:22.516 --> 00:00:25.203
Platform loads the other JDK modules.

8
00:00:25.203 --> 00:00:28.180
And the app loader loads your class path.

9
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:30.473
The request goes to the app loader.

10
00:00:30.473 --> 00:00:33.225
Before it looks anywhere, it asks its parent.

11
00:00:33.225 --> 00:00:35.671
The platform loader asks its parent too.

12
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:37.506
Bootstrap has no Limits.

13
00:00:37.506 --> 00:00:39.213
Platform has none either.

14
00:00:39.213 --> 00:00:42.765
Only then does the app loader search the class path.

15
00:00:42.765 --> 00:00:46.112
It finds Limits in app.jar and defines the class.

16
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.204
This is parent-first delegation.

17
00:00:49.204 --> 00:00:52.373
Ask for String, and bootstrap answers first.

18
00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:55.325
So a stray jar can't replace a JDK class.

19
00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:59.886
The loader that defines a class is part of its identity.

20
00:00:59.886 --> 00:01:03.566
At run time, a class is its name plus its defining loader.

21
00:01:03.566 --> 00:01:08.705
So two loaders that define Limits from the same bytes make two different classes.

22
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.165
Defining the class is only the first of three phases.

23
00:01:13.165 --> 00:01:15.587
Loading, linking and initialization.

24
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:17.821
Loading made a Class object.

25
00:01:17.821 --> 00:01:19.543
None of your code has run.

26
00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:21.078
Linking comes next.

27
00:01:21.078 --> 00:01:24.047
It verifies that the bytecode is type safe.

28
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:27.805
It prepares the static fields with default values.

29
00:01:27.805 --> 00:01:30.424
So CAP exists now, and it holds null.

30
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:34.501
And it resolves names in the constant pool, lazily.

31
00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:37.969
A class that uses a deleted Helper class runs fine.

32
00:01:37.969 --> 00:01:43.000
It throws NoClassDefFoundError only when the branch that uses Helper runs.

33
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.631
Initialization runs the static initializer, <clinit>.

34
00:01:47.631 --> 00:01:52.908
That is every static field initializer and static block, in source order.

35
00:01:52.908 --> 00:01:56.449
CAP becomes 500,000, and the static block prints.

36
00:01:56.667 --> 00:01:58.700
Only this phase runs your code.

37
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.870
It starts on a few precise triggers.

38
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:09.177
A new instance, a static method call, a static field write, or a read of a static field that is not a constant.

39
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.526
A constant read doesn't start it.

40
00:02:11.526 --> 00:02:14.683
Neither does Limits.class, or an array of Limits.

41
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:16.420
So, a check.

42
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.188
Which of these two reads runs the static initializer?

43
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.091
MAX_MINOR is a constant.

44
00:02:22.091 --> 00:02:26.813
javac copied its value into your class, so reading it runs nothing.

45
00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:31.828
CAP is a boxed Integer, not a constant, so reading it runs <clinit>.

46
00:02:31.828 --> 00:02:35.495
Kotlin's const val and plain val split the same way.
